<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-season9" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Season 9: Graphing</title>

  <introduction>
    <p> 
        Equations containing two variables are used to express the relationship between two quantities that affect each other. 
        For example, the formula for the area of a square, <m>A=s^{2}</m>, is an equation containing two variables, which expresses 
        the relationship between the square’s side length and the square’s area. 
      </p>
      <p>
        The graph of an equation containing two variables is a visual representation of that relationship.  
      </p>
      <p>
       In this season, we will learn how to create and read the graphs of equations of two variables. 
      </p>
  </introduction>
